## Problem

`buildEdgeMap` has no `yarn-berry` branch — `graph/index.ts:88` documents the gap as "skip (complex symlink format; fall back to no-op)" and returns an empty `Map`. With no edges, `bfs` visits only the roots themselves (`graph/index.ts:107` `edges.get(name) ?? []`).

## Evidence

With empty edges, `productionSet` = the literal keys of `package.json.dependencies` and `devReachable` = the keys of `devDependencies`. `devOnlySet` (graph/index.ts:145) is therefore exactly the _direct_ devDependency names. Every transitive dev-only package survives the filter at `graph/index.ts:150`.

## Impact

On a yarn berry project `--no-dev` removes a handful of direct devDependencies and keeps their entire transitive closure. The user believes they are seeing a production-only constraint set; they are seeing almost the full tree. Since dev tooling carries the most aggressive `engines.node` floors, the reported safe range is materially wrong and there is no warning that the flag did not work.

## Fix

Yarn berry entries expose `dependencies` under each resolution, so an edge map is buildable with the parser already in use:

    } else if (type === 'yarn-berry') {
      const parsed = parseSyml(content) as Record<string, { dependencies?: Record<string,string> }>
      for (const [key, entry] of Object.entries(parsed)) {
        if (key === '__metadata') continue
        const m = key.match(/^(.+?)@(?:npm|patch|portal|link|file|git):/u)
        if (m === null) continue
        edges.set(m[1], Object.keys(entry.dependencies ?? {}))
      }
    }

If that is deferred, `filterDevPackages` must fail loudly instead of quietly: warn on stderr that `--no-dev` is unsupported for this lockfile type.

## Resolution
Added a yarn-berry branch to buildEdgeMap using parseSyml (already a dependency), keyed by the same protocol regex the berry parser uses, with 'workspace:' added so root workspaces participate. Test asserts webpack -> [tapable] from a berry lockfile. --no-dev now traverses the real graph instead of dropping only direct devDependencies.

## Problem

`npm run cov` reports `intersect.ts` at 84.62% functions / 85.29% branch, with uncovered lines `19-23 27` — that is the entire body of `filterSubsumed` (intersect.ts:15-20) plus the `simplifyComparators` declaration. `filterSubsumed` is the range-reduction step and it is never executed by the suite.

## Evidence

`test/analyzer/intersect.test.ts` contains eight cases and not one input range uses `||`. Every case passes exactly two entries whose normalized forms are distinct and non-subsuming, so the `ranges.some(...)` predicate at intersect.ts:17 never returns true. This is precisely why the Critical OR-concatenation defect ships green: 96/96 tests pass over code that returns `>=18.0.0 <17.0.0-0` for realistic input.

The remaining assertions are weak in the same direction — four of the eight assert only `assert.ok('intersection' in result)` or `assert.ok(result.intersection !== null)`, which hold for any non-crashing implementation and would not catch a wrong range.

## Impact

The module carrying the tool's entire value proposition has tests that verify it does not throw, not that it is correct. Coverage percentage looks healthy (98.18% overall) and conceals it.

## Fix

Add a property-style test that pins semantics rather than shape:

    const SAMPLE = ['14.0.0','16.5.0','17.5.0','18.2.0','20.1.0','22.0.0']
    function assertExact(entries: RangeEntry[]) {
      const { intersection } = computeIntersection(entries)
      for (const v of SAMPLE) {
        const truth = entries.every(e => semver.satisfies(v, e.range))
        assert.equal(intersection !== null && semver.satisfies(v, intersection), truth, v)
      }
    }

and drive it with OR-bearing inputs (`'^17 || ^18'` + `'^16 || ^18'`, `'^14 || ^16 || ^18'` + `'^16 || ^18 || ^20'`) plus a subsumption case (`'>=18'` + `'>=18.2.0'`) that forces `filterSubsumed` to execute. All of these fail against the current implementation — do not weaken them to make them pass.

## Resolution
Added a 'computeIntersection semantics' suite with an assertExact() helper that pins behaviour rather than shape: for every sampled version it asserts the reported range admits it iff every input range does. Driven by OR-bearing and subsumption inputs. intersect.ts coverage went 94.69/85.29/84.62 -> 100.00 line / 90.00 branch / 90.00 funcs; filterSubsumed (the 0%-covered function) no longer exists. Existing weak assertions were left in place, not removed.

## Problem

The same berry-detection heuristic is written twice:

- `detect.ts:12-13` — `readFileSync(lockfilePath, {encoding:'utf8'})` then `head.slice(0, 512).includes('__metadata:')`
- `cli.ts:83-84` — `readFileSync(lockfilePath, 'utf8')` then `content.slice(0, 512).includes('__metadata:')`

Both read the entire lockfile into memory to inspect its first 512 bytes. The variable in `detect.ts` is even named `head`, showing the intent that was not implemented. `cli.ts` then reads the same file a third time at `cli.ts:111` for the actual parse.

## Evidence

`grep -n "__metadata" src/` returns two hits — `src/parsers/detect.ts:13` and `src/cli.ts:84` — carrying byte-identical logic. Neither uses a bounded read: both call `readFileSync` on the full path and only then `.slice(0, 512)`. On the explicit-path branch `cli.ts` reads the file at line 83 and again at line 111 for the parse, so a `yarn.lock` passed by path is read twice in full.

## Impact

Minor but real: a large `yarn.lock` (multi-MB in a monorepo) is fully read two or three times per run for a check that needs one buffer. More importantly the duplication means a fix to the heuristic — Yarn 5 changing its header, say — must land in two places or the explicit-path branch and the auto-detect branch will disagree about the same file.

## Fix

Export the predicate from `detect.ts` and call it from `cli.ts`:

    export function isYarnBerry(lockfilePath: string): boolean {
      let fd: number | undefined
      try {
        fd = openSync(lockfilePath, 'r')
        const buf = Buffer.alloc(512)
        const n = readSync(fd, buf, 0, 512, 0)
        return buf.subarray(0, n).toString('utf8').includes('__metadata:')
      } catch {
        return false
      } finally {
        if (typeof fd !== 'undefined') closeSync(fd)
      }
    }

and replace `cli.ts:83-85` with `isYarnBerry(lockfilePath)`.

## Resolution
isYarnBerry is now exported from src/parsers/detect.ts and reads only a 512-byte header via openSync/readSync with a finally-close, instead of readFileSync on the whole file. cli.ts imports it, removing the duplicated heuristic and one of the two full reads. Three tests cover berry detection, the bounded read (a file with __metadata: past byte 512 is correctly NOT berry), and the missing-file path. detect.ts coverage is now 100% on all four metrics.
